---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { notes } from '../../data/notes';
import { gh, u } from '../../lib/url';

const note = notes.find((n) => n.slug === 'origin')!;
const img = (f: string) => u(`media/notes/origin/${f}`);

// Every date here comes from a commit or a dated design note, mostly in the Backstage app's history.
const timeline = [
  ['12 Aug', 'The first commit to the dev cluster\'s GitOps repo captures the cluster I already had, so ArgoCD can own it from then on.'],
  ['24 Aug', 'Radar is already on my laptop. Claude uses its MCP server to draw me a map of the dev cluster.'],
  ['26 Aug', 'Backstage goes in. The same evening, its first theme is "modeled on radarhq.io".'],
  ['3–4 Sep', 'Installing other people\'s plugins: Argo CD, Kubernetes, Tekton.'],
  ['5 Sep, 4:59 pm', 'The first plugin of my own, a release command centre called Glidepath.'],
  ['5 Sep, 5:43 pm', 'Glidepath is live in Backstage, after one fix to make it show up at all.'],
  ['7 Sep', 'The Hangar brand proposal. Tower takes over as the console the same night.'],
  ['8 Sep', 'The Hangar logo across the whole app. The Glidepath name moves to the CI/CD engine.'],
  ['27 Sep', 'hangarplatform.dev goes live.'],
];
---
<Base title={note.title} description={note.dek} section="notes">
  <div class="wrap">
    <article>
    <header class="hero">
      <p class="eyebrow"><a href={u('notes/')}>Field notes</a> · {note.minutes} min read</p>
      <h1>How Hangar <em>started</em></h1>
      <p class="lede">{note.dek}</p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Two projects, one subscription</h2>
      <p>
        I started two things at once this summer. I wanted to rebuild my home lab and grow my own internal developer
        platform on it, and I wanted to level up my AI skills. It made sense to do the second by doing the first.
      </p>
      <p>
        I'd tried GitHub Copilot in my previous role. The models today are so much better that it
        hardly feels like the same kind of tool. So I signed up for Claude Pro, installed the Claude Code extension for VS Code, and away I went. I had Claude
        read everything I already had, gave it a first spec, and we got going. Honestly, it was mostly vibe coding in
        the beginning. I'd feed it new requirements as they came to me, and every so often one of them would expose a
        crack in the architecture or the implementation. Then we'd back up and head off in a slightly different
        direction. When I took the time to write a more pointed spec, things went noticeably better.
      </p>

      <h2>Finishing the job</h2>
      <p>
        In my previous role I'd assembled a lot of the pieces of my dream platform, and I wanted to finish the job. I
        bought a Mac mini, stood up two Kubernetes clusters (dev and prod), and started installing the essentials. The
        very first commit, on 12 August, simply captures the cluster I already had into Git so that ArgoCD could own it
        from then on. Within a few weeks I was close to where I'd left things before, with a lot of improvements along
        the way.
      </p>
      <p>
        The one piece I'd never had the chance to build was the single pane of glass. So once GitOps and CI/CD were
        working, that's where I turned next.
      </p>

      <h2>Where I started to see the magic</h2>
      <p>
        I put Backstage in on 26 August. At first I made a list of existing plugins I wanted, and had Claude install and
        configure them: Argo CD, Kubernetes, Tekton. It didn't take long for my ambitions to outgrow that list.
      </p>
      <p>
        I know what I like. I know what looks cool and what works. I don't know the first thing about actually
        designing a UI. It turns out I didn't need to. So I asked something like this: "Given what you know about my
        platform so far, and the brief spec I'm about to give you, create some mockups of a new Backstage plugin for
        application release management." A few minutes later I was looking at something I thought were pretty awesome.
      </p>
      <p>
        I picked one. The commit that added it landed at 4:59 on a Saturday afternoon, 5 September, as "a release
        command-center tab", and it was running in my own Backstage within the hour. That night I remember thinking,
        <em>it's like having your own Star Trek replicator.</em> Describe the thing, and there it is. A lot of the time
        since, I've caught myself sitting back with the popcorn, just watching my screen while Claude works his magic.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="shot">
      <img src={img('five-options.webp')} alt="A mockup page titled Making release gates and supply-chain proof first-class, showing Option A Gate Wall and Option B Twin Rail, two of five layouts." width="1600" height="1913" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>
        The habit that stuck: never one design, always several. This one, from 13 September, offered five layouts for
        Tower's active-delivery panel. I picked Option B, Twin Rail, and it shipped the same day. The app name has been
        swapped for one of the Skyport demo apps.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        That became the pattern for every decision after it. Five layouts for a delivery panel. Three ways to show release gates.
        Four ways to draw a logo. Choosing between things I can see is so much easier than describing
        something I can't, and it's cheap enough now that there's no reason not to.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="shot">
      <img src={img('ground-control.webp')} alt="A mockup of Tower's CI/CD tab, labelled Approved direction, with a list of pipeline runs and their mini task graphs above a delivery rail for staging." width="1600" height="1420" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>
        "Ground Control", the CI/CD tab, as an approved mockup. It went into Tower on 11 September and has been refined
        many times since.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Radar had more than a little influence</h2>
      <p>
        Around the same time I came across <a href={u('notes/radar/')}>Radar</a>, the new open source Kubernetes UI. I
        downloaded the desktop app, started using it, and started loving it. Before long I had Claude helping me add
        Tekton and Argo Rollouts features to it.
      </p>
      <p>
        When I went back through the history to write this, I found it ran deeper than I remembered. Radar was already
        drawing maps of my dev cluster on 24 August, two days before Backstage existed. The very first theme I gave
        Backstage, that same evening, is described in the commit as "blue theme modeled on radarhq.io". A week later
        there's another: "Overhaul theme to match Radar's design system". And one of the four logo concepts Claude drew
        for me is literally called <em>Radar Squircle</em>. I'd always suspected Radar planted a seed. The receipts say
        it planted a whole garden.
      </p>

      <h2>From Glidepath to Hangar</h2>
      <p>
        Somewhere in there I asked Claude to give our plugin a name. It came up with <em>Glidepath</em>, and I thought
        that was pretty cool. I didn't draw any connection to Radar yet.
      </p>
      <p>
        I was having so much fun with the mockups that I thought it might be fun to brand the whole platform. I gave
        Claude some direction: a name and a look that stay professional and don't wander into cheesy or campy. It came
        back with a proposal on 7 September: <em>Hangar</em> for the platform, and a family of names around it. That's
        when the aviation language finally clicked for me, and it's the moment I thought, "ahh, maybe Radar had a little
        influence?"
      </p>
      <p>
        Full disclosure: I don't have a thing for planes, no more than the average guy. But the vocabulary is apt, and I
        liked it right away. The names teach you what each piece does instead of decorating it, which was exactly what
        I'd asked for.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="shot">
      <img src={img('brand-proposal.webp')} alt="The Hangar brand system proposal, version 1: Hangar the platform, Glidepath the CI/CD engine, and Tower the release command centre, each with a one-line motto." width="1600" height="1100" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>
        Proposal v1 of the brand, 7 September. "Three names, one hangar door."
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        One twist I'd forgotten until the history reminded me: the proposal moved the names around. The Backstage
        plugin became <a href={u('tower/')}>Tower</a>, one console instead of a tab on every app's page, and I built it
        that same night. <em>Glidepath</em> went to the CI/CD engine, which until then had been called, rather
        less poetically, <code>platform-cicd</code>. By the next day my clusters were pointing at the new name. So the
        first name Claude ever gave me is still here. It just found a better job.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then came the logo, the colours and the icons. We refined them the same way, with lots of options side by side,
        and I adopted <a href={u('notes/diagram-design/')}>the diagram-design skill</a> for every diagram.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="shot">
      <img src={img('logo-concepts.webp')} alt="Logo concepts for review: four ways to draw a hangar door, with Instrument Dial marked as decided, alongside Hangar Arch, Wordmark plus Glyph, and Radar Squircle." width="1600" height="1475" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>
        Four ways to draw a hangar door. The Instrument Dial won and became the <a href={gh('hangar', 'brand/README.md')}>mark
        family</a> you see across this site. Concept D tells you who else was on my mind.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Lessons learned</h2>
      <h3>The big one: managing sessions</h3>
      <p>
        If I could only pass on one lesson, it's this one, and I learned it the hard way. In the first week I kept a
        single Claude session alive for four or five days. That's a terrible idea. A session that long gets slower,
        costs more with every message, and starts to lose track of what it decided at the beginning. I was blowing
        through my hourly <em>and</em> weekly limits all the time.
      </p>
      <p>
        What fixed it was a handoff. When a piece of work reaches a natural break, or the session starts to feel
        heavy, I have Claude write a handoff document: what we're building, what's been decided and why, what's done,
        what's next, and the traps we've already fallen into. Then I close the session, open a fresh one, and its first
        job is to read the handoff. A new session that starts from a good handoff is sharper than an old one that
        remembers everything. I use a handoff skill for this all the time now, and the habit started early: Tower's
        code still cites eight different handoff files from its first few weeks.
      </p>
      <p>
        And they really get you with the extra usage. It's the continue screen on an arcade machine all over again:
        you're deep into the game, the countdown starts, and all it would take is one more quarter. When you're on a
        roll at midnight and the limit hits, it's very tempting to pull out the card and top up just to keep going. Short
        sessions and good handoffs are the best defence I've found, because they make every token count.
      </p>

      <h3>The rest of what I'd tell myself on day one</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Vibe coding is great for discovery and poor for architecture.</strong> Once a decision will be
          expensive to undo, write the spec first. Pointed specs saved me more backtracking than anything else.</li>
        <li><strong>Have it write everything down.</strong> Decisions, dates and reasons belong in the repo, not in a
          chat. Those notes are how I could check this story against what really happened.</li>
        <li><strong>Ask for five, not one.</strong> Mockups cost minutes now. Picking from options beats describing
          what's in your head, especially if, like me, you can't design.</li>
        <li><strong>Taste is still your job.</strong> Claude drew every screen, but knowing what's worth building,
          testing it against real clusters, and saying "no, not that" was still on me.</li>
        <li><strong>Decide what you'll spend before the limit hits</strong>, not at midnight when it does.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <ol class="timeline">
      {timeline.map(([d, t]) => (
        <li><time>{d}</time><span>{t}</span></li>
      ))}
    </ol>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>And then a website</h2>
      <p>
        Eventually I got ambitious enough to buy a domain and put all of this on the web. I'm quite proud of it. At the
        very least it's a record of my work. Maybe no one sees it, or maybe I'll find the courage to share some of
        these essays on LinkedIn. We'll see. Either way, the <a href={u('log/')}>Log</a> will keep growing.
      </p>
      <p class="muted small">
        Dates come from the commit history of the Hangar repos and my Backstage app. The mockups are the real design
        pages Claude made for me at the time, captured as images.
      </p>
    </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>
<style>
  .shot { margin: 2rem 0; max-width: 60rem; }
  .shot img { display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; border: 1px solid var(--rule-strong); border-radius: 8px; }
  .shot figcaption { margin-top: 0.6rem; color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.9rem; max-width: 62ch; line-height: 1.5; }
  .timeline { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 2rem 0; max-width: 52rem; }
  .timeline li { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 9rem 1fr; gap: 0.8rem; padding: 0.6rem 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); line-height: 1.45; }
  .timeline time { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--accent); padding-top: 0.15rem; }
  .timeline span { color: var(--muted); }
  @media (max-width: 560px) { .timeline li { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 0.2rem; } }
</style>
